  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic energy landscape for supramolecular polymerization, showing how the same monomer can follow different assembly pathways and end in different states. A 3D surface plots free energy against reaction progress and concentration, with valleys representing accessible products. One route leads to a thermodynamically stable supramolecular polymer at the deepest minimum, while other routes end in weakly or deeply kinetically trapped polymers that sit in shallower local minima.\n\nColored arrows indicate how these pathways can be controlled. Additives such as chaperones and solvents appear to redirect the system across the landscape, helping it avoid or access certain traps. External stimuli—sonication, stirring, light, and seeds—are shown as another way to push the system out of kinetic traps and toward the stable assembled state. Solid and dashed arrows suggest alternative or stimulus-assisted transitions between minima.\n\nSimple cartoons around the landscape depict the corresponding structures: a monomer block, stacked assemblies, and different packed polymer morphologies. Overall, the figure emphasizes that supramolecular polymer formation is governed not only by thermodynamic stability but also by kinetic trapping, and that pathway selection can be actively tuned by additives or external perturbations.",
